@section 23.3 | Vitamin C

@q 41:9-10

@src 41:11
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 1995, 2018
The best source of Vitamin C is :
(a) Orange
(b) Apple
(c) Carrot
(d) Amla
@ans (d)
@note The source prints the key as (c), but its own explanation says the best source is **amla**; (c) is a misprint.
@end

@src1 41:3
@one
Which vitamin helps in wound healing?
@ans Vitamin C
@end

@q 41:12-13

**Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)** is the **antiscorbutic** vitamin: its lack causes **scurvy** (bleeding gums, slow wound healing). It helps make **collagen**, heals wounds and boosts **iron absorption**. **Amla** (Indian gooseberry) is one of the richest sources, followed by guava, chilli and citrus fruits. It is destroyed by heat and is not stored.

@section 23.4 | Vitamins A, D, E and K

@q 41:24-26

@table
Vitamin | Chemical name | Deficiency | Sources
**A** | **Retinol** | **Night blindness**, xerophthalmia, dry skin | **Carrot, mango, papaya**, green leaves (as **carotene**), liver, fish oil, milk
**D** | **Calciferol** (D_{3} cholecalciferol) | **Rickets** (children), **osteomalacia** (adults) | **Sunlight on skin**, **cod-liver oil**, egg yolk
**E** | **Tocopherol** | **Sterility** (antisterility factor), muscle weakness | **Wheat germ oil**, nuts, seeds
**K** | Phylloquinone (naphthoquinone) | **Delayed blood clotting** (antihaemorrhagic) | **Alfalfa**, green leaves, gut bacteria
@end

