@q 41:45

**Colour blindness** is genetic (X-linked), not a vitamin deficiency. **Rheumatism** is not linked to vitamin A. **Thyroxine** is a hormone, not a vitamin.

@section 23.6 | Energy Foods

@q 41:46-47

@table
Nutrient | Energy per gram
**Fat** | **About 9 kcal** (most)
Carbohydrate | About 4 kcal
Protein | About 4 kcal
Vitamins, minerals, water | **0** (no energy)
@end

@q 41:48-51

**Banana** gives about **90-116 kcal per 100 g**, among the highest of common fruits. **Water** has no calories. **Apples** are rich in **potassium** (good for blood pressure) and soluble fibre (pectin).

@q 41:58

@src1 41:9
@one
Nitrogen is not an essential constituent of
@ans carbohydrates
@end

Carbohydrates (sugars, starch) contain only **carbon, hydrogen and oxygen**; proteins also contain **nitrogen**. Humans cannot digest **cellulose**, which serves only as fibre.

@q 41:63

Basmati grains **lengthen** on cooking (up to twice) because of high **amylose** starch; sticky rice is rich in amylopectin.

@section 23.7 | Proteins and Amino Acids

@q 41:52-57

**Soybean** has the highest protein of common foods (about **40%**); groundnut is rich in both protein (about 25%) and fat (about 45%). **Gluten** in wheat makes dough elastic, giving bread its texture (and triggers **coeliac disease** in some people). Protein needs are higher during growth, pregnancy and lactation and in the elderly to prevent muscle loss.

@q 41:68-72

@src1 41:13
@one
Which protein is present in human hair and nails?
@ans Keratin
@end

Proteins build tissues. **Keratin** forms hair, nails, hooves and the outer skin; **fibroin** is silk; **collagen** is the most abundant protein in the body. Heat, X-rays, acids and heavy-metal salts **denature** proteins; infrared rays mainly warm them. **Cotton** is cellulose. **Enzymes** are biocatalysts; the source accepts both statements, though many enzymes (digestive ones, for example) act away from where they are made.

