@section 23.9 | Milk

@q 41:64-67

@src1 41:10
@src1 41:11
@src1 41:12

@one
The saccharide present in milk is
@ans lactose
@end

@one
The light yellow colour of cow's milk is due to the presence of :
@ans Carotene
@end

@one
The white colour of milk is due to the presence of
@ans casein
@end

@table
Milk component | Fact
**Lactose** | Milk sugar (glucose + galactose); gives sweetness; lactose intolerance in some adults
**Casein** | Main milk **protein** (a phosphoprotein); scatters light and makes milk **white**
**Carotene** | Makes **cow milk yellowish** (buffalo milk is whiter, buffaloes convert carotene to vitamin A)
Fat | Higher in buffalo milk (about 7%) than cow milk (about 4%)
Lacks | **Iron** and vitamin C, so breast-fed babies need iron later
@end

**Saccharin** is an artificial sweetener, not a carbohydrate. A lactating mother needs extra protein: about **70 g a day** was the older standard (ICMR now advises about 60-70 g depending on weight).

@q 41:73-74

@trap
**Funk** named vitamins. **A, D, E, K are fat-soluble**; B and C water-soluble; **C is not stored**, **B_{12} is stored**. **B_{1} = beriberi (polished rice)**, B_{2} = cheilosis, **B_{3} = pellagra**, B_{12} = **cobalt**, pernicious anaemia. **Vitamin K = clotting**, **E = antisterility**, **D = sunlight, rickets**. Best vitamin C source = **amla**. **Fat gives 9 kcal/g.** **Soybean** has the most protein. **Casein** makes milk white, **carotene** yellow; milk lacks **iron**.
@end

@heatmap

@pattern
Vitamins and nutrition give the single largest block of biology questions, nearly every paper from the 1990s to **2024** has at least one. Vitamin-disease matching (2023, 2024) and Assertion-Reason on storage and synthesis (2017, 2019, 2021) are the recurring formats.
@end

